I have everything needed. Here are my findings.

## Findings

### 1. Business-day adjustment logic (production code)

Primary location is the V1 owner controller (there is no dedicated util/service class — the logic lives as a private helper on the controller):

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`

The core method (lines 409–418):
```java
/**
 * Roll a registration date forward onto a business day: when it falls on a Saturday or Sunday,
 * advance it to the following Monday; a weekday is returned unchanged.
 */
private LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate date) {
    while (date.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY || date.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY) {
        date = date.plusDays(1);
    }
    return date;
}
```

Where it is invoked — during owner validation/creation (lines 170–176):
```java
if (owner.getRegistrationDate() != null
    && owner.getRegistrationDate().isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {
    return HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST;
}
LocalDate effectiveDate = owner.getRegistrationDate() != null
    ? owner.getRegistrationDate() : LocalDate.now();
owner.setRegistrationDate(toBusinessDay(effectiveDate));
```

The adjusted (business-day) registration date is then reused by two per-day quota checks in the same file: `isDailyLimitReached` (line 125, defined ~383–406) and `isBulkSignup` (line 293, ~397–406). The field itself lives on the model at `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`.

Note: the production `toBusinessDay` only skips weekends — it does NOT account for public holidays.

### 2. Existing tests for the business-day logic

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` (lines 14–19) — the only actual `@Test`:
```java
@Test
void coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay() throws Exception {
    DayOfWeek dow = LocalDate.parse(
        fetchOwner(createOwnerOk(structuredOwner())).get("registrationDate").asText()).getDayOfWeek();
    assertTrue(dow != DayOfWeek.SATURDAY && dow != DayOfWeek.SUNDAY, dow.toString());
}
```
Its class comment (lines 9–11) explicitly states it asserts only the weekend invariant because the holiday calendar/server clock can't be driven black-box.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — the test base class provides a parallel (test-side) `toBusinessDay` helper (lines 267–274) that DOES skip holidays:
```java
/** Roll a date forward over weekends and pinned holidays to the next business day. */
protected LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate d) {
    while (d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY || d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY
            || HOLIDAYS.contains(d)) {
        d = d.plusDays(1);
    }
    return d;
}
```

### 3. Public-holiday references

There is NO holiday handling in production code. Holidays appear only on the test side, as pinned reference data in `AcceptanceBase.java` (lines 239–242):
```java
/** Fixed public holidays the business-day roll skips (year of the run). */
protected static final Set<LocalDate> HOLIDAYS = Set.of(
    LocalDate.parse("2026-01-01"), LocalDate.parse("2026-01-26"),
    LocalDate.parse("2026-04-25"), LocalDate.parse("2026-12-25"), LocalDate.parse("2026-12-28"));
```
Additional mentions: comment at `AcceptanceBase.java:223` and the class-level comment at `AcceptanceTest.java:9-11`. So the test infrastructure anticipates holiday-aware rolling, but the production `toBusinessDay` implements weekends only — a gap.

### Build tool & how tests run

- Build tool: Maven (`/home/daniel/sandbox/pom.xml`, plus `mvnw`/`mvnw.cmd` wrapper). No Gradle files present.
- The acceptance tests are standard Spring Boot integration tests: `AcceptanceBase` is annotated `@SpringBootTest`, `@AutoConfigureMockMvc`, `@Transactional`, `@WithMockUser(...)` and drives the app in-process via an autowired `MockMvc` (no external server/port needed).
- Run all tests: `./mvnw test`
- Run just this class: `./mvnw test -Dtest=AcceptanceTest`

No files were modified.

